A||N||3||kornholio||What should I do about my tires?||||1
Z||000000||kornholio||10-13-1999||04:36 PM||||The current tires (215/60/14 Defender SRX+4) for my 86 SE hydroplane in wet weather and winter is coming soon.  They are pretty new and have lots of tread left.  Here's my problem.  Should I get a set of thinner snow tires (195/65/14) so that I will have the advantage in the snow and suffer in the rain with my old tires in the spring summer and fall?  OR Should I get a new set of thinner all-season tires to use all year round?  Storing the car over the winter is definately out of the question.<BR>The thing I'm unsure of is the handling of thinner tires on my car that's recommended to use 215's.  Can you actually feel the difference?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-13-1999||06:52 PM||||Your problem isn't tread with as much as tread design. Your current tires have a crappy tread on them. The same size tire with a good tread design likely won't float on every little puddle.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||10-14-1999||12:10 AM||||I've got Perrelli HP's on my formula.  The guy I bought the car from said they worked great, and I've never had a problem with them. An added benefit is they don't cost nearly as much as Goodyear GT+4's.  I got my HP's for just under 80 bucks each when I replaced 'em. Havent had the car in snow yet, we'll see what happens when it comes.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JIM GOOSE||10-14-1999||02:44 PM||||I just bought tires for the rear on my 88 GT,these are 215-60x15. Bought two FALKEN ZIEX H-rated after seeing them on Falkens site.  They are designed like the wet race tires, a wide groove in the middle & diagonal water grooves from middle to edge. Seem better than the GT4s but of course,they are new & old tires were shallow on tread. Cost me $160 mt,bl,valves  & are a great-looking tire.    ||||reg||6
